Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix}$. We want to find $\det A$.
We have
\begin{align*} \det A &= \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - \cos^2 x \cot^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*}
This looks complicated. Let's instead expand directly:
\begin{align*} \det A &= \sec^2 x \left( \cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x \right) - 1 (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + 1 (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x \left( \cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x \right) - \cos^2 x \cot^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x \left( \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} \right) - \frac{\cos^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} (1 - \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*}
Let's compute the determinant of $A$ explicitly.
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \end{align*}
Expand the first term:
\begin{align*} \cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x &= \cos^2 x \cdot \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x = \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} = \frac{\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} \end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} \det A &= \sec^2 x \cdot \frac{\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x)}{\sin^2 x} - \cos^2 x \cot^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*}
Let $x = \frac{\pi}{4}$. Then $\sec^2 x = 2$, $\cos^2 x = \frac{1}{2}$, $\cot^2 x = 1$, $\csc^2 x = 2$, $\cos^4 x = \frac{1}{4}$.
\begin{align*} \det A &= \begin{vmatrix} 2 & 1 & 1 \\ \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ 1 & \frac{1}{2} & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 2 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ \frac{1}{2} & 1 \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 1 & \frac{1}{2} \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 2 \left( \frac{1}{2} - 1 \right) - 1 \left( \frac{1}{2} - 2 \right) + 1 \left( \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{2} \right) \\ &= 2 \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) - 1 \left( -\frac{3}{2} \right) + 1 \left( -\frac{1}{4} \right) \\ &= -1 + \frac{3}{2} - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{-4 + 6 - 1}{4} = \frac{1}{4} \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{1/4}$
