\boxed{16800}
Let $n$ be the number of members in the math club, so $n = 20$. We need to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer such that no member can hold more than one office.
We can select the president from the 20 members. After selecting the president, we have $20-1 = 19$ members remaining.
We can select the vice-president from the remaining 19 members. After selecting the vice-president, we have $20-2 = 18$ members remaining.
We can select the treasurer from the remaining 18 members.
So the total number of ways to select the three officers is the number of permutations of 20 members taken 3 at a time, which is $P(20,3) = 20 \times 19 \times 18$.
Calculating the product:
$20 \times 19 = 380$
$380 \times 18 = 380 \times (10 + 8) = 3800 + 380 \times 8 = 3800 + 3040 = 6840$
Therefore, the number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer is $20 \times 19 \times 18 = 6840$.

Alternatively, we can use the formula for permutations:
$P(n,k) = \frac{n!}{(n-k)!}$, where $n=20$ and $k=3$.
$P(20,3) = \frac{20!}{(20-3)!} = \frac{20!}{17!} = 20 \times 19 \times 18 = 6840$.

Thus, there are 6840 ways to select a president, vice-president, and treasurer from the 20 members of the math club, such that no member can hold more than one office.

